  "query_preview": "..\n(Tonight through next Friday)\nIssued at 1126 PM CST Fri Nov 27 2026\n\nSurface analysis shows high pressure over the Gulf with this\nweekend's low now centered over the Northeast. Dry conditions are\ntherefore expected to prevail through most of Saturday.\nSouthwesterly winds will bring warmer temperatures back into the\nregion with highs climbing back into the upper 50s and low 60s\nthrough Saturday afternoon.\n\nA trough will begin to dig across the central plains late\nSaturday afternoon, aiding in the development of a lee surface\ncyclone over the high plains in western Texas and Oklahoma.\nAnother surface low over Canada will throw a cold front south,\nwhich will intercept the lee cyclone as it moves east towards the\nMid-South Sunday morning. By sunrise Sunday, the surface low will\nbe situated somewhere along the Arkansas/Missouri border with a\ncold front extending south into the Gulf. A 50+ knot low-level\njet will then rapidly advect Gulf moisture ahead of the front and\nbring surface dewpoints into the 60s as far north as southern\nWest Tennessee by noon Sunday. The upper trough will then begin\nto amplify, overspreading the moist surface air mass, with\nRAP/HRRR/RRFS developing 250 - 750 J/kg MLCAPE across the entire\nregion. Although instability will remain meager, a cold front and\nbulk shear values in excess of 50 knots will more than certainly\nmeet the requirements for an organized line of storms, capable of\nmainly damaging winds. 0-3km SRH values do manage to creep up to\naround 300+ m2/s2 ahead of the front, especially in recent\nHRRR/RRFS solutions. This would imply some tornado threat, but\nsample hodographs have shown mostly crosswise shear below 3 km\nthrough Friday. However, both the HRRR/RRFS have trended towards\nmore streamwise-ness within the 00z suite which would increase\nthe tornado threat. Regardless, the SPC has drawn a day\n3 Marginal (level 1/5) Risk for severe damaging winds over\nnortheastern Mississippi for Sunday. Make sure to pay close\nattention to the forecast for Sunday as severe chances could\nchange quickly through the next 36 hours.\n\nThe front will then pass through the region through Sunday\nevening, leading to another cooldown to start next week. Lows\nwill fall back to around freezing Monday and Tuesday, but a\nwarming trend will bring lows back into the 40s by Wednesday with\nhighs returning to the 50s and 60s. The upper pattern becomes\nless amplified and less progressive through much of the week,\nleading to continued mild and dry conditions through at least\nFriday. A new trough will slowly make its way into the southern\nPlains by Friday. Ensembles are generally in good agreement that\nthe trough will make its way into the Mid-South throughout\nFriday, but remain uncertain on the exact amplitude of the\nsystem, keeping forecast confidence low regarding impacts. But\nrecent deterministic runs from the ECMWF and GFS both have ample\nmoisture returning to the region and some instability, hinting at\nyet the potential for another round of severe weather. So, expect\nchanges to the forecast for next Friday, but with the potential\nfor more storms in mind.",
